        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <div><b>Objectives</b></div><div><div>The Artemis missions to the Moon have initiated the development of the Human Landing System (HLS) to transport astronauts to and from the lunar surface. The risk of orthostatic intolerance (OI) and the cardiovascular responses experienced during lunar descent and ascent are important considerations in the development of the HLS. OI is characterized by a group of signs and symptoms that occur after assuming an upright posture, including lightheadedness, dizziness, visual changes, increased heart rate, sudden drops in systolic or diastolic blood pressure, and significant cardiac arrhythmias. Given that OI is a concern during re-exposure to normal gravity (1-G) even after short duration exposures to weightlessness and that crew may be in a standing posture in the vehicle during decent to and accent from the lunar surface, G-loads during the dynamic phases of the Artemis mission may be problematic for some individuals. OI when standing is apparent after just days of exposure to weightlessness and spaceflight analogs, and thus it is expected that OI may be a concern even during lunar descent. Furthermore, it is unlikely that the partial gravity exposure during stays on the lunar surface will be protective against OI during ascent from the surface.</div><div><br></div><div>There were no reports of symptoms consistent with OI during the Apollo missions. However, all the Apollo crewmembers were male with a history high performance aircraft experience, and almost all the commanders demonstrated high heart rates during sustained G-levels ranging from 0.4- to 0.6-G over ~9 min during lunar descent and ascent. The G-levels to which the Artemis astronauts will be exposed have not been defined, but it is likely that the G-levels will be greater and/or the exposures will be longer than those experienced by Apollo astronauts based upon the flight plan required to reach Gateway, which will be at a higher orbit than the Apollo Command and Service Module. Further, the proportion of women in the astronaut corps has been increasing and it has been publicly stated that the first Artemis mission to the lunar surface will include at least one woman; thus the effect of sex on the cardiovascular responses to acceleration should be considered with regard to lunar descent and ascent. In general, women have lower orthostatic tolerance and are more susceptible to post-spaceflight orthostatic intolerance when re-exposed to 1-G. However, it is unclear whether women also would be less able to the tolerate lower levels of acceleration that likely will be experienced during descent to and ascent from the lunar surface.</div><div><br></div><div>Lower body compression garments are one of the countermeasures that have been used to protect against OI after exposure to weightlessness. Specifically, NASA has developed the Orion Orthostatic Intolerance Garment (OIG) for the Artemis missions, but the suit's efficacy has not been demonstrated in women and men during long exposures (20 minutes) to simulated G-levels (0.5-, 0.75, 1.0-G) that astronauts may experience during lunar descent and ascent during the Artemis missions.</div></div><div><br></div><div><div>This study has the following aims</div><div><br></div><div>1. To determine whether there are differences between hypovolemic men and women in orthostatic tolerance (assessed by tilt time) and cardiovascular responses during 20 minutes of exposure to a range of gravity levels (0.50-, 0.75-, and 1.00-G) simulated by different angles of head-up tilt that Artemis astronauts might experience during descent to and ascent from the lunar surface.</div><div>2. To determine whether the Orion Orthostatic Intolerance Garment (OIG) will protect men and women against orthostatic intolerance (assessed by tilt time) and reduce the cardiovascular responses caused by exposure to the range gravity levels (0.50-, 0.75-, and 1.00-G) simulated by different angles of head-up tilt that Artemis astronauts might experience during descent to and ascent from the lunar surface.</div></div><div><br></div><div><b>Approach</b></div><div><div style="">19 men and 19 women will be recruited to complete this study. The participants will be similar to the astronaut population along important characteristics such as age, body mass index, and physical fitness. To complete the study, subjects will participate in two phases of testing.</div><div style=""><br></div><div style="">Phase I will evaluate differences in OI between the sexes (Aim 1). During the first of two laboratory visits (1A), test operators will obtain anthropometric measurements to be used in the design and construction of an individualized Orion OIG, familiarize the subjects to the tilt test procedures, and measure the subject’s baseline plasma volume (carbon monoxide rebreathing method) and tolerance to 80° head-up tilt (1.00-G). During the second of two laboratory visits (2A), plasma volume will be measured prior to inducing hypovolemia through administration of a furosemide infusion (20 mg). Once hypovolemia is achieved, participants will be exposed to three 20-minute sessions of head-up tilt tests at 30° (0.50-G), 50° (0.75-G), and 80° (1-G) in a randomized order counterbalanced across subjects. During each tilt test, measurements that will be obtained while supine and during head-up tilt include brachial blood pressure, beat-to-beat finger blood pressure, and heart rate (ECG) and ultrasound parameters required to calculate stroke volume, cardiac output and total peripheral resistance. Hematocrit, hemoglobin, and carboxyhemoglobin will be measured from blood samples and used to calculate red blood cell volume, total blood volume, and plasma volume. Blood samples will be collected prior to each tilt test to calculate changes in plasma volume across the test day.</div><div style=""><br></div><div style="">Phase II also will consist of two laboratory visits. During the first visit (2A), subjects will don the OIG constructed from the anthropometric measures obtained in visit 1A to ensure correct sizing. During the second visit (2B), plasma volume will be measured using the same carbon monoxide rebreathing method prior to inducing hypovolemia through administration of furosemide infusion. After hypovolemia is achieved, subjects will don the OIG, and will be assessed for orthostatic tolerance with compression garments using the same tilt test protocol as in phase I, with G-levels presented in the same order in which they were in visit 1B to avoid any potential confounding effects of order when comparing tilt results within subjects.</div></div><div style=""><br></div><div style=""><b>Results</b></div><div style="">It is expected that females will be more susceptible to OI and demonstrate a greater heart rate response when hypovolemic than males at 1-G (Phase I) and that differences between sexes (tolerance time and cardiovascular responses) will be less as simulated G-levels decrease. The findings from this phase of the study will provide additional detail to the HLS acceleration limits during the dynamic flight phases when astronauts are standing but not wearing compression garments. However, it is expected that all subjects will be able to tolerate the entire tilt duration when hypovolemic and using lower body compression garments (Phase II). The findings from this phase of the study will provide additional detail to the HLS acceleration limits during the dynamic flight phases when astronauts are standing, but wearing compression garments, particularly with regard to the duration of tolerance.</div>
        </div>

